"Imagine a ship far out in the middle of the ocean. There are no streets, no signs, and no buildings, only water in every direction. If the ship needs help, how can the sailors tell anyone where they are? On land we use addresses with street names and house numbers. The ocean has none of these. Luckily, the whole Earth has an address system of its own, and it needs only two numbers.": "1bdb2d09ffc4",
"Imagine a ship far out in the middle of the ocean. There are no streets, no signs, and no buildings, only water in every direction. If the ship needs help, how can the sailors tell anyone where they are? On land we use addresses with street names and house numbers. The ocean has none of these. Luckily, the whole Earth has an address system of its own, and it needs only two numbers. The first number is latitude. Picture a line around the middle of the Earth, halfway between the North Pole and the South Pole. This is the equator. Latitude tells you how far north or south of the equator a place is. It is measured in degrees, from 0 at the equator to 90 at each pole. Lines of latitude are circles that run east and west. They are also called parallels, because they never meet, and they get smaller as they get closer to the poles. The second number is longitude. Lines of longitude, also called meridians, run from the North Pole to the South Pole. One of them is chosen as the starting line and is called the prime meridian. It passes through Greenwich, in London. Longitude tells you how far east or west of the prime meridian a place is, from 0 degrees up to 180 degrees. Unlike lines of latitude, all lines of longitude meet at the two poles. Put the two numbers together and you have an address. Latitude is said first, then longitude. The middle of Taiwan is at about 23.5 degrees north, 121 degrees east. For more exact work, each degree is divided into 60 smaller parts called minutes. One degree of latitude is about 111 kilometers on the ground wherever you are. One degree of longitude is about 111 kilometers at the equator, but it shrinks toward the poles, where all the lines come together. Why does longitude start at Greenwich? The equator is set by the way the Earth spins, so nobody had to choose it. The prime meridian is different. Any line from pole to pole could have been the starting line, and for a long time different countries used different ones. In 1884, delegates from 25 countries met in Washington, D.C., and voted to use the line through the observatory at Greenwich, which most ships' charts already used. The prime meridian is an agreement among people. For sailors, latitude was the easy number. In the Northern Hemisphere, the height of the North Star above the horizon is about the same as your latitude. Longitude was much harder, because finding it means knowing the exact time. The Earth turns 15 degrees every hour, so a clock that keeps the time of a known place can tell a ship how far east or west it has sailed. In the 1700s, the English clockmaker John Harrison built clocks that could do this at sea. Today the satellites of GPS give your phone both numbers in a few seconds.": "608805508702",
